            'authority_url' => null,
            'eligibility_html' => '<p>The baseline entry requirement for Indian nationals is itself disputed across sources (visa-free vs. visa-on-arrival vs. visa-required) — this conflict was found independently in this research and is disclosed rather than resolved.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Family Reunification Visa Category</h4><p>A long-stay guide lists a family reunification visa among Guinea-Bissau\'s long-stay visa types, based on a proven marital or parental relationship. One source specifically states that the spouse and minor children of a Lusophone citizen domiciled in Guinea-Bissau may enter under the family-reunification framework defined by law.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Entry vs. Residence</h4><p>One guide distinguishes the entry visa (which allows arrival) from the residence permit (obtained after arrival from the Directorate General of Migration and Borders or the National Directorate of Migration and Aliens).</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Dependent Documents and a Conflict on Process</h4><p>Dependents typically need marriage certificates (spouses) or birth certificates (children), and the primary holder must show financial capacity to support them. However, sources conflict on process: one says dependents must apply separately for a residence visa, while another implies dependents are covered under the sponsor\'s permit. This is disclosed as an open conflict.</p><p>Official, centralized guidance on this route is difficult to find; confirm current rules with the Guinea-Bissau embassy/consulate or immigration authorities.</p>',
            'indian_applicant_html' => '<h4 style=\'margin:0 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Who Applies</h4><p>You would need to establish, and evidence, a marital or parental relationship with a sponsor who is a Lusophone citizen (or otherwise qualifying resident) domiciled in Guinea-Bissau. The entry visa is sought before travel; the residence permit is pursued after arrival.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>What You (the Applicant) Provide</h4><ul><li>Valid passport</li><li>Marriage certificate (spouse) or birth certificate (child)</li><li>Entry visa application, if your baseline entry category requires one</li></ul><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>What Your Sponsor Separately Needs</h4><ul><li>Proof of their own citizenship/residence status in Guinea-Bissau</li><li>Evidence of financial capacity to support you as a dependent</li></ul><p>Because sources disagree on whether you must file separately for the residence visa or are covered under your sponsor\'s permit, confirm the current process with the Directorate General of Migration and Borders or the nearest Guinea-Bissau mission before applying.</p>',
            'seo_title' => 'Guinea-Bissau Family Visa for Indians | What We Know',
