            'eligibility_html' => '<p>Per the project\'s standing finding from earlier phases, Guyana has a genuine, unresolved baseline visa-free-vs-visa-required conflict for Indian ordinary-passport holders (most sources say visa-free tourist entry, one flags a separate eTA/eVisa requirement); this family-visa research does not resolve that conflict.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>No Standalone Family Reunification Program Found</h4><p>One guide states that Guyana does not appear to publish a fully separate, codified, public-facing \'Family Reunification Visa\' program page; family reunification is instead handled through a mix of entry visa rules, border admission, and extensions.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Dependents of Work-Permit Holders</h4><p>A relocation guide says work-permit holders can typically sponsor a spouse and dependent children, but must show they can financially support them and provide adequate housing, with a marriage certificate among the listed documents. This is a commercial, non-official source.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Visitor Visa on Arrival for Accompanying Family</h4><p>A Guyanese consulate notice states that the spouse or family of a foreign national working in Guyana is issued a Visitor Visa on Arrival if accompanying that worker. This is a short-stay visit provision, not a residence route, and should not be assumed to permit long-term settlement.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Permanent Residence for Foreign Spouses of Citizens</h4><p>One aggregator guide says foreign spouses of Guyanese citizens are likely candidates for permanent residence, with another unofficial source suggesting a 2-5 year timeline; neither figure is confirmed from an official source.</p><p>Confirm current requirements with Guyana Immigration Support Services, the Ministry of Home Affairs, or the Guyanese High Commission in New Delhi.</p>',
            'indian_applicant_html' => '<h4 style=\'margin:0 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Who Applies</h4><p>For a short visit accompanying a working spouse, the Visitor Visa on Arrival appears to be issued at the border. For dependent status tied to a work permit, the sponsoring employer/worker is typically involved. For permanent residence as the spouse of a Guyanese citizen, the application would be filed with Guyanese immigration authorities.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>What You (the Applicant) Provide</h4><ul><li>Valid passport</li><li>Marriage certificate (spouse) or birth certificate (dependent child)</li><li>Evidence of the relationship to the Guyana-based sponsor or spouse</li></ul><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>What Your Sponsor Separately Needs</h4><ul><li>Proof of their own work permit or citizenship/residence status in Guyana</li><li>Evidence of financial capacity and adequate housing to support dependents</li></ul><p>Given the thin and partly conflicting evidence on both baseline entry and the family route, confirm current requirements directly with the Guyanese High Commission in New Delhi before proceeding.</p>',
            'seo_title' => 'Guyana Family Visa for Indians | No Standalone Scheme',
