            'official_visa_name' => 'Iceland Family Reunification Residence Permit (fjölskyldusameining) — distinct from the short-stay Family Visit Schengen Visa',
            'visa_subclass_code' => 'Schengen Type C (short-stay family visit only)',
            'intro_html' => '<p>Iceland has two genuinely different routes that should not be conflated. A short FAMILY VISIT uses the ordinary Schengen Type C visa, applied for at the Embassy of Iceland in New Delhi through VFS Global, allowing up to 90 days in any 180-day period. Long-stay FAMILY REUNIFICATION is a separate residence-permit process run by the Directorate of Immigration (Útlendingastofnun), available to the spouse, cohabiting/registered partner, children under 18, and parents aged 67 or older of a sponsor who holds a qualifying Icelandic residence permit.</p>',
            'typical_stay' => 'Family visit (Schengen Type C): up to 90 days in any 180-day period; Family reunification residence permit: multi-year, tied to the sponsor\'s own permit, renewable',
            'entry_type' => 'Short-stay Schengen Type C visa for a family visit; separate long-stay residence permit for family reunification',
            'processing_time_text' => 'Family visit Schengen visa: estimates conflict across sources, ranging from about 15 days to 2-6 weeks — apply well in advance; family reunification residence permit processing time not independently confirmed',
            'validity_text' => 'Family visit: up to 90 days within a 180-day window; family reunification residence permit: tied to and generally mirrors the sponsor\'s underlying permit category and duration',
            'application_method' => 'Family visit: applied for at the Embassy of Iceland, New Delhi, via a VFS Global visa application centre in India; Family reunification: application process via the Directorate of Immigration (Útlendingastofnun)/island.is, generally initiated once the sponsor holds a qualifying residence permit in Iceland',
            'interview_required' => 'Not independently confirmed for either route',
            'biometric_required' => 'Required for the Schengen family-visit visa, per standard Schengen practice; not independently confirmed for the residence-permit route',
            'government_fee_text' => 'Family visit Schengen visa fee reported inconsistently across commercial sources (figures cited range roughly from INR 8,000-12,500, or around EUR 80) — confirm the current fee with the embassy/VFS before paying; family reunification permit fee not independently confirmed',
            'application_centre' => 'VFS Global visa application centre in India (for the Schengen family-visit visa); Directorate of Immigration (Útlendingastofnun) / island.is (for the family reunification residence permit)',
            'authority_name' => 'Directorate of Immigration (Útlendingastofnun), Iceland',
            'authority_url' => 'https://island.is/en/o/directorate-of-immigration/have-applied-family',
            'eligibility_html' => '<h4 style=\'margin:0 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Family Visit (Short Stay)</h4><p>A visit to relatives or friends in Iceland uses the Schengen Type C visa. Per embassy FAQ guidance reflected in commercial sources, this requires a formal invitation letter from the host along with proof of the host\'s residence status, and a private-accommodation proof where the applicant will stay with family. This is a visit visa only — it does not grant any right to settle or work in Iceland.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Family Reunification (Long-Stay Residence)</h4><p>Per island.is (the official Icelandic government portal) and secondary guides, family reunification covers a spouse or cohabiting/registered partner, children under 18, and parents aged 67 or older, where the Iceland-based relative acts as sponsor. Not every sponsor permit type carries reunification rights — guides note that permits for labour shortage, specialized staff, and student permits are specifically said to carry reunification rights, implying other permit categories may not.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Work Rights Differ by Relationship to the Sponsor</h4><p>Per island.is, a marital spouse of an Icelandic citizen may begin working as soon as a residence-permit application has been submitted and the fee paid. By contrast, a marital spouse of a foreign national, and a cohabiting (unmarried) partner of an Icelandic citizen, may not begin working until the residence permit has actually been granted — a meaningful distinction between spouses of citizens versus of foreign residents, and between married and cohabiting partners.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Staying in Iceland During Processing</h4><p>Per island.is, someone who was already legally staying in Iceland when the residence-permit application (as a spouse or child) was submitted is generally allowed to remain while it is processed, with exceptions where the sponsor holds or applied for a permit based on studies or a collaboration/service agreement.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Income/Housing Requirements</h4><p>Secondary guides state the sponsor must show sufficient income and housing, and that the Directorate of Immigration publishes a current monthly reference income amount, but the exact current figure for the family-reunification route was not independently confirmed. (A separate, unrelated route — subsidiary-protection family reunification — publishes a minimum support figure of ISK 247,572/month for an individual and ISK 396,115/month for a married couple, per island.is; this figure is cited only as a loosely related reference point and should not be assumed to apply to the standard sponsor-income test.)</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Spouse of a Refugee</h4><p>A spouse of a person granted international protection in Iceland is entitled to international protection through family reunification unless special reasons apply, per island.is — a distinct, narrower route from ordinary family reunification.</p>',
            'indian_applicant_html' => '<h4 style=\'margin:0 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Who Applies — Family Visit</h4><p>You apply in India at a VFS Global centre for the Schengen Type C visa, submitting your own documents alongside your Iceland-based host\'s invitation letter and proof of residence status.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Who Applies — Family Reunification</h4><p>The residence-permit application process is generally handled through the Directorate of Immigration (Útlendingastofnun)/island.is once your sponsor holds a qualifying Icelandic residence permit; exact filing mechanics (whether you apply from India or your sponsor initiates it from Iceland) were not independently confirmed.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>What You (the Applicant) Provide</h4><ul><li>Valid passport</li><li>Marriage/partnership certificate or birth certificate establishing the relationship</li><li>Invitation letter from your host/sponsor (for a family visit)</li><li>Travel health insurance (commonly cited for the Schengen visa)</li></ul><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>What Your Sponsor Separately Needs</h4><ul><li>Proof of their qualifying Icelandic residence permit (noting not all permit types carry reunification rights)</li><li>Evidence of sufficient income and housing in Iceland</li></ul><p>Because the two routes (visit vs. reunification) are genuinely different processes with different documents, fees, and outcomes, our consultants can help you identify which one actually fits your situation before you apply.</p>',
            'seo_title' => 'Iceland Family Visa for Indians | Visit vs Reunification',
            'meta_description' => 'Iceland family visit vs family reunification for Indian nationals — Schengen Type C visa rules, residence-permit sponsor conditions, via VisaAgency.in.',
            'og_title' => 'Iceland: Family Visit Visa vs Family Reunification',
            'og_description' => 'A short Iceland family visit and long-stay family reunification are different processes with different sponsors, documents, and outcomes — here is how.',
