            'interview_required' => 'Not independently confirmed',
            'biometric_required' => 'Not independently confirmed',
            'government_fee_text' => 'Visa fee payment receipt is a listed requirement on consulate pages, but the current fee amount was not independently confirmed',
            'application_centre' => 'Consulate General of the Islamic Republic of Iran, Mumbai or Hyderabad (in India); E-Visa online portal for initial registration',
            'authority_name' => 'Consulate General of the Islamic Republic of Iran, Mumbai / Hyderabad (mfa.ir)',
            'authority_url' => 'https://mumbai.mfa.gov.ir/en/GeneralCategoryServices/15392',
            'eligibility_html' => '<h4 style=\'margin:0 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Family (Marital) Visa — Registered Spouse Only</h4><p>Per the Mumbai and Hyderabad consulate pages, this visa is specifically for the foreign spouse and children of an Iranian citizen whose marriage is registered in Iran. The applicant must upload an image of the Iranian spouse\'s identity booklet (shenasnameh) showing the names of the husband and children. An applicant holding a non-Indian passport must also provide their Indian residence card (visa or OCI). Minors require a legal guardian\'s consent.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Unregistered Marriages Are Treated Differently</h4><p>Per Iranian mission pages (Croatia/Zagreb), a marriage that is not registered in Iran cannot use the Family (Marital) Visa and must instead use an entry (Relatives) visa — a materially different, more cautious treatment than for a registered spouse. No evidence was found that Iran recognizes unmarried or informally cohabiting partners for any family-entry category; this should be assumed not available unless specifically confirmed by the consulate.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Relatives Visa — Broader Family Visits</h4><p>For visiting Iran-based relatives generally (not necessarily a spouse), the applicant\'s relative or a travel agency in Iran must first obtain a grant notice (visa reference number) from the Ministry of Foreign Affairs Passport & Visa Office, which is then submitted to the consulate along with the application. Applicants should not approach the consulate before receiving this grant notice, per mission guidance.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Dependents of a Work-Permit/Residency Holder in Iran (Unofficial Guide)</h4><p>A third-party relocation guide (not an official mfa.ir source) states that foreign nationals holding a valid Iranian work permit and residency permit may be eligible to sponsor immediate family members through the Foreign Ministry or an Iranian embassy abroad; family members reportedly must then register and obtain their own residency permits after arrival, and dependents are generally not permitted to work in Iran. This was not confirmed on an official mfa.ir page and should be treated as indicative only.</p>',
            'indian_applicant_html' => '<h4 style=\'margin:0 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Who Applies</h4><p>You (the Indian applicant) register online via Iran\'s E-Visa system and then submit documents to the Consulate General in Mumbai or Hyderabad. For the Relatives Visa, your Iran-based relative must act first, obtaining the grant notice before your application can proceed.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>What You (the Applicant) Provide — Family (Marital) Visa</h4><ul><li>Original passport valid for at least 6 months</li><li>Completed E-Visa application and fee payment receipt</li><li>Image and original of your Iranian spouse\'s identity booklet (shenasnameh) showing your and your children\'s names</li><li>Your Indian residence card (OCI/visa) if you hold a non-Indian passport</li><li>Legal guardian\'s consent for minors</li></ul><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>What You (the Applicant) Provide — Relatives Visa</h4><ul><li>Original passport valid for at least 6 months</li><li>E-Visa application form and fee payment receipt</li><li>The grant notice (visa reference number) obtained by your Iran-based relative</li><li>Notarized guardian consent letter, for applicants under 18</li></ul><p>Because these are two genuinely different visas with different document sets, confirm which one fits your actual relationship (registered spouse vs. visiting relative) before applying at the Mumbai or Hyderabad consulate.</p>',
            'seo_title' => 'Iran Family Visa for Indians | Marital vs Relatives Visa',
