            'government_fee_text' => 'Baseline e-Visa fee reported inconsistently (one source cites around USD 160 including mandatory health insurance, another cites a USD 50 government fee); Kurdistan Region spousal residency card fee not independently confirmed',
            'application_centre' => 'evisa.iq online portal (baseline entry); Kurdistan Regional Government Directorate General of Residency (Kurdistan Region spousal residency card, in-country)',
            'authority_name' => 'Kurdistan Regional Government (KRG) Directorate General of Residency',
            'authority_url' => 'https://service.gov.krd/en/node/1609',
            'eligibility_html' => '<p>Most sources describe a mandatory e-Visa for baseline entry (around 30 days), with one unreliable outlier claiming visa-free entry — the majority view is treated as more reliable here, but this is not independently verified from the official evisa.iq page itself.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Kurdistan Region Spousal Residency Card (Officially Documented)</h4><p>The Kurdistan Regional Government\'s own service page (service.gov.krd) describes a residency card for a married couple where one spouse must be an Iraqi citizen. If the foreign spouse is outside Iraq, they must first obtain a pre-entry visa before the residency card process can begin in-country. The card is valid for 1 to 3 years, issued under the Foreigners Residency Law No. 76 of 2017. This route applies specifically to the Kurdistan Region, not to federal Iraq generally — an important regional distinction.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Federal Iraq — Dependents of a Work/Residency Permit Holder (Unofficial Guide)</h4><p>A third-party relocation guide (Rivermate, not an official Iraqi source) states that a foreign worker holding a valid residency and work permit in Iraq can typically sponsor a spouse, with an authenticated marriage certificate as a required document. No official confirmation of this route, its fees, or its processing was found for federal Iraq.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Unmarried Partners</h4><p>No evidence was found of any route recognizing unmarried or cohabiting partners in either federal Iraq or the Kurdistan Region; the documented route explicitly requires marriage to an Iraqi citizen.</p><p>Given how thin and region-specific this evidence is, confirm current requirements with the Kurdistan Regional Government (for the Kurdistan Region) or Iraq\'s federal Ministry of Interior/evisa.iq (for the rest of Iraq) before proceeding.</p>',
            'indian_applicant_html' => '<h4 style=\'margin:0 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Who Applies — Kurdistan Region Spousal Residency</h4><p>If you are outside Iraq, you first obtain a pre-entry visa; the residency card application itself is then pursued in-country, with your Iraqi-citizen spouse\'s status as the qualifying link.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>What You (the Applicant) Provide</h4><ul><li>Valid passport</li><li>Marriage certificate, likely requiring authentication/legalization</li><li>Pre-entry visa, if applying from outside Iraq</li></ul><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>What Your Sponsor (Iraqi-Citizen Spouse) Separately Needs</h4><ul><li>Proof of Iraqi citizenship</li><li>Compliance with Foreigners Residency Law No. 76 of 2017 requirements, as administered by the KRG Directorate General of Residency</li></ul><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Federal Iraq (Outside Kurdistan Region)</h4><p>No officially confirmed family-residence process was located; if your sponsor is a foreign worker (not an Iraqi citizen) holding a residency/work permit, a third-party guide suggests sponsorship may be possible with an authenticated marriage certificate, but this needs direct confirmation with Iraqi authorities.</p><p>Given the regional split and thin federal-level evidence, we recommend confirming your specific route with the relevant authority before applying.</p>',
