            'official_visa_name' => 'Libya Family/Dependant Residency for Spouse, Minor Children, Unmarried Daughters and Dependent Parents of a Foreign Resident (under Law No. 17 of 1962 and Law No. 6 of 1987)',
            'visa_subclass_code' => null,
            'intro_html' => '<p>Libya does not appear to operate a separately branded \'Family Visa\' product; instead, dependant residency is addressed within its general foreigners\'-residency legislation. Under Law No. 17 of 1962 (per security-legislation.ly), wives, minor children, unmarried daughters, and dependent parents of a foreign national may be granted the same residency status as the sponsoring foreign resident. Law No. 6 of 1987 covers residency visas and related duties more broadly, and reportedly requires dependants named on an entry visa to register with the passports authority within 7 days of arrival. In practice, sponsors (commonly an employer acting on behalf of a sponsoring employee) are reported to file entry-visa applications for dependants from abroad with the Ministry of Interior, followed by residency-permit applications once the dependants arrive in Libya. This entry addresses strictly the formal family-residency eligibility framework; it does not address Libya\'s wider security or diplomatic situation, which this project records and discloses separately and keeps out of this formal visa-eligibility assessment per the project\'s standing rule. A short family VISIT under Libya\'s baseline entry-visa rules is a separate matter from this residency route, and this project has recorded unresolved conflicts in earlier phases over whether Indian ordinary-passport holders need a visa at all for short stays to Libya; that baseline conflict is not re-resolved here. Secondary aggregator sources also disagree on some family-route details (for example, one cites a 60-month initial stay for a family route, while another marks its family-reunification requirements as unverified).</p>',
