            'eligibility_html' => '<p>Macau does not have a generic "Family Visa" - family entry is linked entirely to the sponsor\'s own Temporary Residency category (there is no independent family-reunification channel for a sponsor who is only a Macau permanent resident without an underlying qualifying permit, based on the sources reviewed).</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Sponsor Status Required</h4><ul><li>The principal applicant/sponsor must hold (or be applying for) Macau Temporary Residency - for example, as an investor or under the "management and technical personnel with special qualifications" category</li><li>Only sponsors whose salaries/qualifications meet IPIM\'s criteria as skilled workers are generally allowed to bring dependent spouses and children to live in Macau, per secondary reporting (Macau Daily Times)</li></ul><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Eligible Dependants</h4><ul><li>Spouse (registered marriage)</li><li>De facto (unmarried) spouse - IPIM documentation references a marriage record "or" de facto spouse category, suggesting Macau does give some recognition here, but the exact evidentiary threshold was not independently confirmed</li><li>Minor children under 18</li></ul><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Work Rights</h4><p>Secondary reporting indicates a family-dependent permit generally does NOT allow the dependant to work in Macau, though this was not confirmed against an official IPIM statement.</p><p>This is general guidance, not a guarantee of approval - IPIM and the Public Security Police Force assess each case individually, including genuine-cohabitation checks at renewal (as illustrated by a Macau court ruling on a challenged renewal).</p>',
            'indian_applicant_html' => '<h4 style=\'margin:0 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Who Applies</h4><p>Your Macau-resident sponsor (the Temporary Residency permit holder) applies to IPIM for your "family dependent" status; this is a sponsor-initiated application, not one you file independently from India.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Documents You Will Need</h4><ul><li>Valid Indian passport</li><li>Marriage certificate (or, for a de facto spouse, equivalent relationship proof) - for an Indian applicant this would need to be the equivalent of the Chinese-nationality marriage record IPIM\'s checklist describes, adapted for Indian documentation; this was not spelled out for Indian nationals specifically in the sources reviewed</li><li>Certificate of no criminal record</li><li>Employment statement, if you intend to work in Macau while on dependent status (restrictions may apply)</li></ul><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>What Your Sponsor Must Separately Provide</h4><p>A bank guarantee or surety (the guarantor must be a Macau permanent resident or a local company), proof of their own qualifying Temporary Residency status, and - where the sponsor works in Macau - evidence their salary/qualifications meet IPIM\'s family-dependent criteria.</p><p>Our consultants can help confirm which Indian documents IPIM will accept as equivalents and how to prepare the guarantee requirement.</p>',
