            'eligibility_html' => '<h4 style=\'margin:0 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Family Reunification (Residence Route)</h4><p>Under the Law on Foreigners, a ‘sponsor’ is defined as any foreigner residing legally in North Macedonia applying for family reunification. One UNHCR-sourced definition (specific to protection-status holders, not ordinary residents) lists family as spouse, unmarried partner, unmarried minor children, and — if the sponsor is a minor — parents; whether this exact definition extends to ordinary (non-refugee) residents was not independently confirmed. General guidance distinguishes temporary residence (up to around 1 year, extendable) from permanent residence (requiring about 5 years of uninterrupted temporary residence).</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Short-Stay Entry for Indian Passport Holders</h4><p>This is unresolved. Some sources state a visa is mandatory for Indian citizens visiting for tourism/business, with no visa-on-arrival or eVisa option. Other sources, including a page claiming a 2026 update, state Indian passport holders may enter visa-free for up to 90 days. A third, lower-reliability source claims a free visa is issued on arrival, which contradicts both other claims. VisaAgency.in should not assert a single position on this without direct confirmation from the Ministry of Foreign Affairs of North Macedonia or its mission in India.</p>',
