            'eligibility_html' => '<h4 style=\'margin:0 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Spouse/Dependant of a PNG Citizen</h4><p>Covers spouses and dependants of a PNG citizen wanting to live in PNG temporarily. Holders are not permitted to engage in paid employment. ICA may require a financial bond or supporting evidence of means.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Spouse/Dependant of a Permanent Resident or Resident (Long Term) Holder</h4><p>A dependant entry permit is granted only to live in PNG with the Resident (Long Term) permit holder, and dependants must generally be included in the principal’s initial application. Sources conflict on work rights here: one ICA page states employment is prohibited for dependants (with an exception allowing a permanent resident’s spouse to work in a family-run business), while a separate ICA FAQ states the spouse of a Permanent Residency holder may work — this conflict within ICA’s own published material was not resolved and should be confirmed directly with ICA.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Unmarried (De Facto) Partners</h4><p>De facto partners must provide statutory declarations from both parties regarding the duration of the relationship, plus a signed undertaking not to seek employment. Relationships under 12 months’ standing receive only a short-term permit (up to 12 months), rather than the standard 5-year grant — so unmarried partners are explicitly treated differently depending on relationship duration, and this should not be assumed equivalent to the spousal route.</p>',
